gateway.nodes.denyCommands
WhatsApp heartbeat/debounce configuration
older model catalog references

openclaw doctor --fix successfully migrated most of the configuration.

A valid recovered configuration was then installed as:

~/.openclaw/openclaw.json

Validation was performed with:

openclaw config validate

Result:

Config valid

A configuration backup before running migration tools is strongly recommended.

Issue 3 — OpenClaw 2026.7.2-beta.6 Had No Matching Official Plugin Builds

After the core was upgraded to:

2026.7.2-beta.6

Gateway status reported official plugin drift.

The affected plugins included:

Brave
DeepSeek
llama.cpp
WhatsApp

The Gateway expected:

2026.7.2-beta.6

but matching plugin versions could not be installed.

Direct npm checks confirmed:

openclaw@2026.7.2-beta.6
    EXISTS

@openclaw/whatsapp@2026.7.2-beta.6
    NOT PUBLISHED

@openclaw/deepseek-provider@2026.7.2-beta.6
    NOT PUBLISHED

@openclaw/brave-plugin@2026.7.2-beta.6
    NOT PUBLISHED

@openclaw/llama-cpp-provider@2026.7.2-beta.6
    NOT PUBLISHED

This meant the Gateway's recommended command to update plugins to the exact core version could not succeed.

Attempts similar to:

openclaw plugins update \
  @openclaw/whatsapp@2026.7.2-beta.6

returned:

Package not found on npm
Important Upgrade Hazard

At the same time, npm showed that the generic core beta tag had advanced beyond this release:

openclaw beta
→ 2026.8.1-beta.1

while the relevant plugins still had:

plugin beta
→ 2026.7.2-beta.7

Therefore this would have been risky:

openclaw update --channel beta

because it could move the core farther ahead of the plugins.

Resolution

We identified 2026.7.2-beta.7 as the first common release where the required official plugin 
packages existed.

The core was explicitly aligned to:

2026.7.2-beta.7

instead of blindly following the moving beta dist-tag.

Issue 4 — Brave and llama.cpp Continued Following Old ClawHub Install Sources

After moving the core to 2026.7.2-beta.7:

DeepSeek:  2026.7.2-beta.7
WhatsApp:  2026.7.2-beta.7

were correctly aligned.

However:

Brave:      2026.7.1
llama-cpp:  2026.7.1

remained behind.

Gateway status identified them as originating from:

clawhub

Ordinary commands such as:

openclaw plugins update brave
openclaw plugins update llama-cpp

continued following their previously recorded install source.

Resolution

The plugins were explicitly reinstalled from npm and pinned to the exact OpenClaw version:

openclaw plugins install \
  npm:@openclaw/brave-plugin@2026.7.2-beta.7 \
  --force --pin
openclaw plugins install \
  npm:@openclaw/llama-cpp-provider@2026.7.2-beta.7 \
  --force --pin

Runtime inspection subsequently showed:

packageVersion:            2026.7.2-beta.7
version:                   2026.7.2-beta.7
builtWithOpenClawVersion:  2026.7.2-beta.7
status:                    loaded
source:                    npm

for all four official plugins.

The plugin drift warning disappeared.

Issue 5 — Gateway Appeared Unstable After Everything Was Correctly Aligned

After the plugin issue was fixed, Gateway status intermittently showed:

Runtime: running

Read probe: failed

connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:18789

and sometimes:

WebSocket close 1006

At other times:

ss -ltnp | grep ':18789'

showed the Gateway listening normally:

127.0.0.1:18789
[::1]:18789

and:

curl http://127.0.0.1:18789/

returned:

HTTP 200

The Control UI also successfully connected and executed WebSocket RPC requests.

This initially looked like a Gateway networking or authentication regression.

It was not.

Diagnostic Breakthrough — The Gateway Was Being Intentionally Restarted

Systemd reported:

Result=success
NRestarts=0
ActiveState=active
SubState=running

This was critical.

NRestarts=0 indicated the service manager was not restarting the Gateway because of crashes.

The journal instead showed repeated clean shutdowns:

Stopping openclaw-gateway.service
[admission] closed: restart drain
[gateway] received SIGTERM; shutting down
[shutdown] completed cleanly
Started openclaw-gateway.service

The pattern repeated approximately every 50–55 seconds.

Example pattern:

Gateway starts
~53 seconds
Gateway receives SIGTERM
Gateway shuts down cleanly
Gateway starts
~53 seconds
Gateway receives SIGTERM

This established that an external process was deliberately executing a Gateway restart.

Issue 6 — Custom WhatsApp Watchdog Was Restarting a Healthy Gateway

The following user-level systemd timer was discovered:

openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer

Its configuration included:

[Timer]
OnBootSec=60
OnUnitActiveSec=50
AccuracySec=5s
Unit=openclaw-wa-healthcheck.service

The associated service executed:

~/.local/bin/openclaw-wa-healthcheck.sh

The journal showed:

WhatsApp bridge down; restarting openclaw-gateway

followed immediately by the Gateway shutdown.

This exactly matched the unexplained restart timestamps.

Actual Script Bug

The watchdog still contained the obsolete OpenClaw path:

BIN="~/.npm-global/bin/openclaw"

That installation had already been removed during the earlier duplicate-CLI cleanup.

The new OpenClaw executable was:

/usr/bin/openclaw

The watchdog logic was essentially:

STATUS="$(${BIN} channels status --probe 2>&1)"
RC=$?

if [ ${RC} -ne 0 ]; then
    # Assume WhatsApp is down
    RESTART=1
fi

Therefore:

old OpenClaw executable missing
          ↓
health-check command fails
          ↓
nonzero exit status
          ↓
script assumes WhatsApp failed
          ↓
systemctl --user restart openclaw-gateway
          ↓
Gateway temporarily unavailable
          ↓
watchdog runs again
          ↓
repeat

The OpenClaw Gateway itself was healthy.

Additional Feedback Loop

During repeated reconnects the WhatsApp plugin also reported:

Failed hydrating participating groups on connect:
Error: rate-overlimit

The evidence does not establish that this was the original cause of the restart problem.

A reasonable inference is that repeatedly reconnecting the WhatsApp client every minute 
aggravated the provider-side rate limiting.

The restart watchdog therefore potentially created a self-reinforcing loop:

health-check failure
      ↓
Gateway restart
      ↓
WhatsApp reconnect
      ↓
metadata/group hydration
      ↓
rate-overlimit
      ↓
next watchdog probe
      ↓
restart again
WhatsApp Access Control Was Not the Problem

The WhatsApp plugin itself loaded correctly.

The final configuration continued to enforce:

DM policy: allowlist
Allowed senders:
    <REDACTED-ALLOWED-NUMBER-1>
    <REDACTED-ALLOWED-NUMBER-2>

Group policy: disabled

Runtime logs confirmed:

Listening for WhatsApp inbound messages
(DM + groups disabled by groupPolicy)

No sensitive telephone numbers should be included in a public issue report.

Watchdog Fix

The immediate fix was to stop the faulty watchdog:

systemctl --user stop openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer

and prevent it from automatically returning until corrected:

systemctl --user disable openclaw-wa-healthcheck.timer

The script should use:

BIN="/usr/bin/openclaw"

instead of the deleted user-local npm path.

More importantly, a health-check command failure should be treated as:

UNKNOWN

rather than:

WHATSAPP DOWN

A failed diagnostic command should not automatically restart the entire Gateway.

The corrected watchdog should only restart after multiple explicit WhatsApp failures and should include a restart cooldown.

Revised Watchdog Frequency

The original timer checked every: 50 seconds

This was unnecessarily aggressive for a WhatsApp self-healing mechanism.
The timer has now been changed to 15 minutes or 4 checks per hour, 
This substantially reduces unnecessary probing and reconnect/restart pressure.

A reasonable recovery strategy is Check every 15 minutes, Else perform one controlled recovery.

